\paragraph{Practical rule.} Before tightening a threshold, compute $\sqrt{3p(1-p)}$ for the resulting flag rate. If it lies below the acceptance floor, the experiment is uninformative: recalibrate the band, or keep the looser operating point.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Exact Spearman reading.} Compute $d_i=\sigma(i)-\tau(i)$, accumulate $D=\sum d_i^2$ and $F=\sum|d_i|$, then $\rho=1-6D/(n^3-n)$. Time $O(n)$, space $O(1)$; exact in rational arithmetic. By Theorem~\ref{thm:parity} an odd $D$ signals an implementation bug (ties, or a non-bijective rank assignment).

\paragraph{(B) Inversion count by merge sort.} Naive counting is $\Theta(n^2)$; merge sort counts inversions in $O(n\log n)$, since taking an element from the right half while $k$ elements remain in the left contributes exactly $k$ inversions. The output feeds the certificates $F\le 2\inv$ and $D\le 2(n-1)\inv$.

\paragraph{(C) Ceiling audit.} Given $m,n$ and an acceptance floor $c$, evaluate $\sqrt{3m(n-m)/(n^2-1)}$ and compare with $c$; if smaller, report the operating point as structurally infeasible, and by bisection on $p$ report the largest flag rate at which the floor is attainable. Time $O(1)$ per evaluation, $O(\log(1/\varepsilon))$ for the bisection.

\paragraph{(D) Exhaustive validation.} Enumerating $\Sn$ for small $n$ confirms the quantisation set, the diameter, the null mean and the metric inequalities; this is $O(n!\cdot n)$ and practical to $n=8$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the geometry buys}

Everything in Sections 3--6 is a consequence of the single fact that the $n!$ rank vectors lie on one sphere inside one hyperplane: the chordal representation, the diameter, the Pearson identity, the parity invariant, the rigidity gap, and the exact null mean. This explains why these facts are exact rather than asymptotic---they concern a finite, highly symmetric point configuration on which $\Sn$ acts transitively.

Section 7 is harder: the Diaconis--Graham upper bound is not a cosphericity consequence, requiring the two-sided displacement count and the double-counting identity. Its content is that the $\ell^1$ geometry of the permutohedron is controlled by the Coxeter combinatorics of $\Sn$.

Section 8 required a change of model: the threshold axis cannot be represented as a perturbation of a permutation at all. Modelling it as a two-block variable is the step that makes the ceiling appear, and the ceiling is sharp because the extremal block sums are attained.

\subsection{An open problem: the Diaconis--Graham lower bound}

The companion inequality
\[
\inv(\sigma)+T(\sigma)\ \le\ F(\sigma),
\]
where $T(\sigma)$ is the Cayley length (the minimum number of transpositions expressing $\sigma$, equal to the number of non-fixed points minus the number of nontrivial cycles), is not established here. Two natural approaches fail for identifiable reasons. \emph{Bubble-sort induction} stalls because an adjacent transposition can leave $F$ unchanged while decreasing $\inv$ by one, so the induction has no slack. \emph{Per-index charging} stalls because the dual of Lemma~\ref{lem:right}, namely $|\Invr(\sigma,i)|\le(\sigma(i)-i)^+$, is false: for $\sigma=[2,3,1,0]$ at $i=2$ the left side is $1$ while $\sigma(2)-2=-1$.

A genuinely global argument is required. The inequality has been checked exhaustively for all $n\le6$. The most promising reading is structural: $F$, $\inv$ and $T$ are all length functions on $\Sn$, and the claim is that $F$ dominates the sum of the Coxeter length and the Cayley length. Since $F$ is subadditive with $F((a\,b))=2|a-b|$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:Fsub}, \ref{thm:Fswap}), one has strong control over $F$ along transposition factorisations, and it is plausible that the inequality is a facet inequality of the permutohedron viewed through the Cayley graph. Establishing it would close the sandwich, giving mutual bi-Lipschitz equivalence of the three classical ranking metrics with explicit constants.

\subsection{Limitations}

The theory assumes tie-free rankings. Ties place the rank vector on a face rather than a vertex, modifying the parity invariant and the rigidity gap; the appropriate object is then the permutohedron of a composition. The block ceiling degrades gracefully: with mid-ranks assigned to ties the extremal block sums remain valid up to a correction of order $t/n$ for $t$ tied items.

The block ceiling is stated for a single threshold. For a $k$-block coarsening the analogous ceiling depends on the block-size profile and interpolates between the two-block value and $1$.

Finally, the results concern \emph{attainability}, not estimation: they say what a correlation can be, not what it will be under a given sampling design. That is exactly what makes them usable in advance of an experiment.

\subsection{Broader connections}

The permutohedron is the weight polytope of the standard representation of $\Sn$ and the Cayley graph polytope of the Coxeter system $(\Sn,\{s_i\})$; its edges are adjacent transpositions and its normal fan is the braid arrangement. Reading rank statistics as functions on this polytope embeds classical nonparametric statistics into that framework. The block ceiling is an instance of a general \emph{coarsening bound}: projecting onto a low-dimensional quotient of the vertex set caps every correlation with the original. Analogous ceilings should exist for quantile bucketing, top-$k$ truncation and censoring.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The Diaconis--Graham lower bound as a facet inequality.} Read $\inv(\sigma)+T(\sigma)\le F(\sigma)$ as the assertion that $F$ dominates the sum of the Coxeter and Cayley length functions on $\Sn$. Subadditivity of $F$ and the exact transposition cost supply control along transposition factorisations; a global exchange argument is the missing ingredient.
\item \textbf{$k$-block and quantile coarsening ceilings.} Generalise the block ceiling to a $k$-block bucketing with profile $(m_1,\dots,m_k)$. The extremal fibre-sum argument goes through verbatim; the right normalisation should recover $3p(1-p)$ at $k=2$ and $1$ at $k=n$, giving an operational ``resolution versus attainable correlation'' curve.
\item \textbf{Ties and the permutohedron of a composition.} Extend the parity invariant and the rigidity gap to tied data, where rank vectors lie on faces. The expected gap is $12/\bigl(n^3-n-\sum_t(t^3-t)\bigr)$, matching the classical tie correction, with a geometric derivation.
\item \textbf{Quantisation and exact tests.} Since $\rho$ takes only $O(n^3)$ distinct values, exact permutation tests on small $n$ have an atomically supported null distribution. Combining exact unbiasedness with the quantisation grid should yield exact, non-asymptotic critical values.
\item \textbf{Dynamic thresholds.} The block ceiling is static, but in practice $u$ drifts and $p$ with it. Concavity of $p\mapsto3p(1-p)$ suggests the average ceiling is bounded by the ceiling of the average flag rate, making a fluctuating threshold strictly worse than its mean.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

On finite, tie-free data a rank correlation is not an arbitrary number in $[-1,1]$. It is a rescaled chordal distance between two vertices of the permutohedron, and the polytope dictates its behaviour: attainable values form an even-spaced grid; there is a forbidden window of width $12/(n^3-n)$ just below $1$; the exact diameter is $n(n^2-1)/3$, achieved only at antipodal rankings; the uniform ensemble has null mean exactly zero; and the $\ell^1$, $\ell^2$ and inversion readings are equivalent up to explicit constants. Most consequentially, coarsening the data by a threshold caps the correlation at $\sqrt{3p(1-p)}$, whatever the underlying statistic. A dial that is inert to population size but breaks systematically under a tighter threshold is not exhibiting two separate phenomena: scale does not move the vertices of the permutohedron, but coarsening collapses them.

